    "id": "yeats-innisfree",
    "mk": "w-b-yeats",
    "kind": "Poem",
    "title": "The Lake Isle of Innisfree",
    "maker": "W. B. Yeats",
    "date": "1890 (National Observer, Dec 1890); collected 1892 (The Countess Kathleen and Various Legends and Lyrics)",
    "poem": "I will arise and go now, and go to Innisfree,\nAnd a small cabin build there, of clay and wattles made;\nNine bean-rows will I have there, a hive for the honey-bee,\nAnd live alone in the bee-loud glade.\n\nAnd I shall have some peace there, for peace comes dropping slow,\nDropping from the veils of the morning to where the cricket sings;\nThere midnight's all a glimmer, and noon a purple glow,\nAnd evening full of the linnet's wings.\n\nI will arise and go now, for always night and day\nI hear lake water lapping with low sounds by the shore;\nWhile I stand on the roadway, or on the pavements grey,\nI hear it in the deep heart's core.",
    "poemNote": "whole",
    "deeper": "",
    "story": [
     "In 1888 the young W. B. Yeats was living in London and homesick for Sligo. Walking down Fleet Street, he saw a little fountain in a shop window, a ball balanced on its jet of water, and it brought back the lakes of his childhood. Since his teens he had dreamed of living like Thoreau on Innisfree, a small island in Lough Gill. The poem first appeared in The National Observer in December 1890.",
     "Sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Lake_Isle_of_Innisfree · https://headstuff.org/culture/history/irish-history-history/alake-isle-innisfree-wb-yeats/"
    ],
    "gloss": {
     "wattles": "Woven sticks, plastered with clay to make walls.",
     "glade": "An open clearing in a wood.",
     "linnet": "A small brown songbird."
    }
