    "mk": "augustine-of-hippo-translated-by-j-g-pilkington",
    "kind": "Passage",
    "title": "Confessions, Book I, chapter 1",
    "maker": "Augustine of Hippo, translated by J. G. Pilkington",
    "date": "c. 397–400; Pilkington translation 1876",
    "quote": "Great art Thou, O Lord, and greatly to be praised; great is Thy power, and of Thy wisdom there is no end. And man, being a part of Thy creation, desires to praise Thee, — man, who bears about with him his mortality, the witness of his sin, even the witness that Thou \"resistest the proud,\" — yet man, this part of Thy creation, desires to praise Thee. Thou movest us to delight in praising Thee; for Thou hast formed us for Thyself, and our hearts are restless till they find rest in Thee.",
    "cite": "Confessions, Book I, chapter 1, c. 397–400; Pilkington translation 1876",
    "deeper": "",
    "story": [
     "Augustine wrote the Confessions in the last years of the fourth century, not long after becoming bishop of Hippo, a port town in North Africa. He did not write it as a memoir told to readers but as a long prayer spoken to God, and each book begins in prayer. These are its very first lines: before he tells a single fact about his life, he praises, and names the restlessness that the rest of the book will trace.",
     "Sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confessions_(Augustine) · https://www.britannica.com/biography/Saint-Augustine"
    ],
