    "mk": "christina-rossetti",
    "kind": "Poem",
    "title": "Goblin Market (closing lines)",
    "maker": "Christina Rossetti",
    "date": "1862 (Goblin Market and Other Poems)",
    "poem": "\"For there is no friend like a sister\nIn calm or stormy weather;\nTo cheer one on the tedious way,\nTo fetch one if one goes astray,\nTo lift one if one totters down,\nTo strengthen whilst one stands.\"",
    "poemNote": "closing 6 lines",
    "deeper": "",
    "story": [
     "Christina Rossetti wrote Goblin Market in the spring of 1859, in her notebooks long before it was printed. Her brother Dante Gabriel suggested the title, replacing her own \"A Peep at the Goblins,\" and drew the pictures for the volume when it appeared in 1862. Around the time she wrote it, she began volunteering at a refuge in Highgate for women who had fallen on hard times. Rossetti resisted reading the poem as an allegory; it is, at heart, a story of two sisters.",
     "Sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goblin_Market · https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/christina-rossetti"
    ],
