    "id": "blake-auguries-of-innocence",
    "mk": "william-blake",
    "kind": "Poem",
    "title": "Auguries of Innocence",
    "maker": "William Blake",
    "date": "written c. 1803 (Pickering Manuscript); first published 1863 (with Gilchrist's Life of William Blake)",
    "poem": "To see a World in a Grain of Sand\nAnd a Heaven in a Wild Flower\nHold Infinity in the palm of your hand\nAnd Eternity in an hour\n\nA Robin Red breast in a Cage\nPuts all Heaven in a Rage\nA Dove house filld with Doves & Pigeons\nShudders Hell thr' all its regions\n\nA dog starvd at his Masters Gate\nPredicts the ruin of the State\nA Horse misusd upon the Road\nCalls to Heaven for Human blood\n\nEach outcry of the hunted Hare\nA fibre from the Brain does tear\nA Skylark wounded in the wing\nA Cherubim does cease to sing",
    "poemNote": "lines 1–16 (of 132)",
    "deeper": "",
    "story": [
     "William Blake copied \"Auguries of Innocence\" into a fair-copy notebook, probably while living at Felpham on the Sussex coast in the early 1800s. He never published it. The poem first appeared in 1863, decades after his death, in a companion volume to Alexander Gilchrist's Life of William Blake. The notebook later took the name of a man who acquired it, and is known as the Pickering Manuscript. An augury is a sign or omen.",
     "Sources: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auguries_of_Innocence · https://rpo.library.utoronto.ca/content/auguries-innocence"
    ],
    "gloss": {
     "augury": "Omen, a sign read for meaning.",
     "Dove house": "Dovecote, where pigeons are kept.",
     "Cherubim": "An angel (strictly the plural of cherub)."
    }
